  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 238 PM EDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis:\n\nRidging encompassing the Midwest and Great Lakes will begin to fold \nover far southeast Canada and New England through the first half of \nthis week, building surface high pressure across these areas as \nwell. Strong shortwave troughing will then punch across the region \nin the early Wednesday/Thursday timeframe. Ample forcing aloft will \ndeepen the associated surface cyclone as it treks across southern \nCanada, eventually working over Hudson Bay by Wednesday and across \nQuebec Thursday. The attendant cold front stretching back southwest \nlooks to swing across the Great Lakes late Wednesday. Ridging is \nthen expected to slide overhead late this week, temporarily building \nhigh pressure in through Friday before additional troughing slides \noverhead next weekend.\n\nForecast Details:\n\nPleasant weather will remain this afternoon and tonight to round out \nthe holiday weekend. Temperatures may climb a few more degrees into \nthe upper 70s/low 80s for places underneath mostly sunny skies \nbefore cooling back into the mid 50s to low 60s overnight.\n\nThe primary focus of the forecast period will be on the return of \nrain chances and breezy conditions Tuesday through the middle of the \nweek. Rain-free weather will likely continue for most areas through \nTuesday morning before chances increase from the west during the \nafternoon. A dry low-level airmass in place ahead of the \naforementioned front may make it difficult for initial light rain to \nreach the ground through the afternoon. However, at least some light \nrain will be possible across northern Michigan during the \nafternoon/evening.\n\nBest chances/coverage of rain is expected late Tuesday evening into \nWednesday morning as the profile becomes increasingly saturated as \nthe front works closer. A few embedded thunderstorms will be \npossible during this time, but nearly moist adiabatic profiles \nevident in forecast soundings with very little buoyancy extending \npast -10C will keep thunderstorm chances low overall. Winds will \nalso increase Tuesday night into Wednesday out of the southwest \nahead of the front, likely gusting to 25-40 mph through the \nafternoon. Winds and rain chances will taper Wednesday evening after \nthe front swings through.\n\nCooler temperatures will lie in the wake of this system Thursday \nwith afternoon temperatures looking to stay in the upper 60s to low \n70s. Dry weather is anticipated through the second half of the week \nbefore at least some low-end chances return this upcoming weekend. \nThat said, current thought is that the weekend appears more dry than \nwet, potentially bringing a more substantial stretch of largely rain-\nfree weather across the area into the following week.",
